# Metal–organic frameworks catalyzed C–C and C–heteroatom coupling reactions

## Introduction and scope of the review
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have recently emerged as versatile materials in the field of heterogeneous catalysis.1–9 In these materials, metal ions or clusters of metal ions are coordinated to rigid bi or multipodal organic linkers10–12 defining a crystalline framework with high porosity. One of the main characteristics of MOFs is their high metal content and frequently the metal ions have free coordination positions that can act as Lewis acids13–17 to bind with substrates, in addition to acting as redox sites18,19 in oxidation–reduction reactions. Another important property of MOFs with respect to their applications in catalysis is their high surface area and open porosity. In fact MOFs are together with covalent organic frameworks (COFs) among the solids with the lowest framework density, i.e., with the lowest mass of the unit cell and, therefore, with a considerable empty space in the structure. This high porosity implies that they should exhibit the lowest mass transfer limitations. A high surface area and the presence of active sites are the prerequisites for a solid to act as heterogeneous catalysts, but there are other necessary conditions that can also be met by MOFs. One of these is structural stability20–22 under the reaction conditions including reagents, solvents, temperature and pressure. Crystal stability has been frequently suggested as one of the main drawbacks for the use of MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis.20 While it is true that not all the reported MOFs are stable upon storage or upon use as catalysts, there are by now several structures such as MIL-101, UiO-66 and others that are extremely robust and can survive even treatments with strong Bro¨nsted acids or hydrothermal conditions at high temperature for long duration. These features of MOFs including high proportion of active sites, porosity and stability in combination with easy and reliable preparation procedures and the possibility to synthesise these materials by design make MOFs highly promising heterogeneous catalysts. The last concept of MOFs by design is related to the fact that knowing the nature of metal nodes, their coordination geometry and the directionality of the organic linker, it is possible to predict the structure of a MOF before its synthesis.23 As it has been demonstrated in many cases, replacement of one linker by other with identical directionality in the binding positions, but larger size, leads to the formation of isostructural MOFs24 with large pore dimensions and higher porosity.25–30 Fig. 1 summarizes some of the features of MOFs with relevance for their use as heterogeneous catalysts.
All the above unique properties found in MOFs have made these materials highly promising in heterogeneous catalysis31–36 and photocatalysis.37 The reader is also referred to the existing literature for a deeper coverage of MOF synthesis, possible geometry of the pore system in MOFs their applications as heterogeneous catalysts in reactions such as oxidations,18 preparation of fine chemicals,38 synthesis of nitrogen-containing heterocycles39 and the condensation reactions of carbonyl groups4 among other possibilities.
In addition to exploiting the intrinsic catalytic activity of MOFs based on their constitutive components due to their large pore volume, MOFs can also be used as host materials to incorporate metal–metal oxide nanoparticles (NPs). These hosted NPs inside MOF can act as active sites in reactions such as hydrogenations or reductions, oxidations and coupling reactions.41 In these cases, the framework of MOFs is embedding and
