In the present review, we will focus on those reports describing the use of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts to promote crosscoupling C–C, C–X $\left(\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{O},\mathbf{N}\right)$ reactions. These types of reactions are frequently carried out using Pd complexes or salts in solution in combination or in the absence of other metals as a co-catalyst. Although some of these Pd complexes having special bulky phosphines or other ligands have been found to be extremely active to promote the reaction even with deactivated or notably unreactive substrates, the difficulty in the separation of Pd from the reaction mixture and the competitive advantage of recovery and reuse of the catalyst has motivated the search for heterogeneous catalysts. Common problems encountered in heterogeneous Pd catalysts include two possibilities to promote these cross-coupling reactions, either by the intrinsic activity of the metal nodes or by incorporating metal NPs will be described.
