The purpose of this review is to demonstrate that there are sufficient data showing that MOFs can serve to develop extremely efficient heterogeneous catalysts for these types of organic reactions.
## Cross-coupling reactions
C–C and $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{X}$ couplings are among the most versatile reactions in organic synthesis42 because they can be used to form new bonds with carbon under extremely mild conditions,43–49 the reaction being compatible with many different organic functional groups including carbonyl, hydroxyl, nitro, cyano, multiple bonds and others. Most of these cross-coupling reactions have been reported to be promoted by Pd salts and complexes.50 In fact, development of Pd complexes exhibiting high activity in cross-coupling reactions is one area on its own in homogeneous catalysis that has developed intensively since the 80’s.51–58 The outcome of this research is that the catalytic activity of Pd complexes depends considerably on the nature of the ligands that determines the electronic density around the Pd atom, the biting angle for interaction with the substrate and the stability of the catalyst during the mechanistic cycle. Among the most important ligands, we can mention biaryl phosphines (Buchwald ligand),59–61 phosphine based pincer ligands,62 nitrogen-containing heterocyclic carbenes,63,64 carbapalladacycles65 and others.
These organometallic Pd complexes can be extremely efficient to promote C–C cross-coupling reactions and they can be used in proportions as low as $0.01\mathrm{\mol\%}$ for substrates that exhibit low reactivity due to the strength of the bond involved in the reaction or the steric hindrance around the active sites. These Pd ligands can catalyze in the homogeneous phase different types of reactions such as Suzuki–Miyaura, Mizoroki–Heck, Sonogashira, Stille, Ullmann reactions among others (Fig. 2).
The major problem of homogeneous catalysis is that although it can be extremely efficient, it is difficult to operate under continuous flow and the reaction mixture becomes contaminated with Pd metal. For many syntheses of drugs and other fine chemicals for human consumption, the Pd content has to be below the ppm level66 and this condition complicates considerably the purification of the reaction mixture after the reaction using homogeneous Pd catalysis. These drawbacks of homogeneous catalysis have triggered a research line trying to convert homogeneous systems into heterogeneous catalysts.67 One of the simplest straightforward approaches to heterogenize a homogeneous catalyst is to anchor a suitable derivative onto a large surface area support. For this purpose, an organic polymer but more especially inorganic porous materials including mesoporous silicas have been widely used.68 However, the main problem of these approaches has been the lack of stability of Pd complexes that under the reaction conditions tend to decompose leading to the formation of Pd NPs. This decomposition is due to the chemical stress to which the Pd complex is subjected during the course of reaction that requires the swing of Pd between $^{+2}$ to 0 oxidation state in the oxidative addition and reductive elimination steps of the reaction mechanism (Fig. 3).
If the oxidative addition of $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ is slower compared to aggregation of $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ atoms, then appearance of Pd NPs starts to occur.
As a consequence, it has been found that although a fresh solid catalyst may contain the Pd complex exhibiting high activity and the initial reaction rate, soon the formation of Pd NPs having still some activity starts to develop. The key point is that Pd NPs are also able to promote cross-coupling reactions69,70 although their intrinsic activity is commonly much lower than that of organometallic complexes and decreases upon increase of the dimension of Pd NPs. Accordingly, the catalytic activity of supported Pd complexes starts to decay as the complexes decompose and Pd NPs grow in dimension.
The above considerations have led to the general conclusion that it should be important to develop solid catalysts containing stable small Pd NPs rather than unstable Pd complexes. In these solid catalysts the growth of the NP dimension should be impeded. This approach has the advantages of not requiring the synthesis and anchoring of expensive organic ligands. The fresh catalyst is already a solid in which Pd NPs of small size of a few nm have been already formed, but their size is controlled by interaction with the solid support. Among the various possibilities to avoid particle growth, one that has been frequently exploited is to include metal NPs in the cages/cavities of porous materials. There are precedents in the literature reporting the use of clays,71 zeolites72 and porous silicas73 as supports for Pd NPs. Not surprisingly, after the synthesis of MOFs and due to their large porosity, the use of these materials as hosts for metal NPs including $\mathbf{Pd}^{41}$ has become intensively investigated and this will be one of the topics in the present review. As commented earlier, MOFs to be used as hosts for this purpose have to be sufficiently robust to stand the reaction conditions without leaching Pd to the liquid phase and keeping constant its particle size.
One point that is still not fully understood is how the interaction of support–host with Pd NPs modulates their catalytic activity. However, it is a general fact that most of the heterogeneous Pd catalysts are much less active than homogeneous complexes and the target in this area is to develop more active solid catalysts that can promote the coupling of substrates with low reactivity such as aryl chlorides or 2-substituted aryl bromides. In the particular case of MOFs, this higher activity could be developed by designing a host MOF material with adequate ligands and metal nodes to fine tune the activity of Pd. The possibility to introduce substituents in the organic linkers such as amino groups or to have linkers with hetero atoms can result in more active Pd/MOF catalysts. Considering the large diversity of MOFs that can in principle be obtained, this approach is doable and is likely to lead to the development of highly active catalysts that have not been possible to be obtained using porous silicas or aluminosilicates. In addition, the catalytic activity of Pd NPs for cross-coupling reactions can be modified by the preparation of core–shell NPs or alloy NPs combining Pd with other metals. In this way, the electronic density of catalytic relevant NPs could be optimized to match the requirements of organic reactions. In fact, one issue in cross-coupling reactions is the requirement of Pd that is a costly noble metal. It would be important to optimize the activity per Pd atom by preparation of suitable alloys or core–shell NPs. In this regard, the use of widely available and affordable Ni either to form alloys with Pd or even to promote cross-coupling reactions in the absence of Pd should be a target in this area. It should be commented that there are examples on the use of Ni salts and complexes as catalysts for Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reactions74 but there is a continuous need in the development of more general and efficient heterogeneous catalyst based on Ni in combination or not with Pd trying to expand its use to other types of coupling reactions.
Finally, as a general goal in MOF catalysis, the target in the area should be to employ MOFs as cross-coupling catalysts in the synthesis of organic molecules of large commercial relevance. While there are many commercial processes carried out on a large scale in oil refining and petrochemistry based on the use of zeolites as catalysts, there are still no industrial processes based on MOFs, probably because these materials are considerably more novel and recent than zeolites. Application of MOFs as catalysts for cross-coupling reactions on an industrial scale will increase the interest on these materials proving that they can stand the conditions required for economically viable processes.
## Best practices in the use of MOFs as catalysts in cross-coupling reactions
One of the main problems in heterogeneous catalysis that also appears in this area is how to compare the catalytic efficiency of different materials. Since the reactivity of substrates for coupling reactions depends considerably on the aryl halide and the presence of substituents as well as reaction conditions, it is not always straightforward to draw conclusions about the relative catalytic activity between materials studied under different conditions. Thus, catalysts that can work very efficiently to promote Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling of iodobenzene may be inefficient or comparatively less active for the same reaction with bromobenzene or chlorobenzene. No clue about the reactivity of a catalyst for bromobenzene or chlorobenzene can be obtained by its behaviour for iodobenzene and, therefore, screening of a series of substrates is always necessary to rank the catalytic activity of a material. It has been proposed that the coupling of 4-bromoanisole with phenylboronic acid should be considered as a bench mark reaction to test catalytic activity of Pd containing materials.75 In addition to the C–Br bond of this substrate, the presence of an electron-donating methoxy group leads to the deactivation of this compound for C–C coupling reactions. Therefore, a good practice in this area is to provide data not only for iodobenzene but also for a variety of substrates including 4-bromoanisole and also aryl chlorides having substituents in the $o$ -position that are among the less reactive substrates for this type of reactions.
Besides showing the scope of the catalyst, another good practice is to determine the stability of the catalyst upon recycling. As commented briefly earlier, it is known that the catalytic activity of the fresh material can deactivate, among other possible reasons by increasing the Pd particle size. Accordingly, catalytic data of recyclability should not be performed only for iodobenzene but also for more challenging substrates since it could happen that catalysts that are reusable in several cycles for iodobenzene are not reusable for other more challenging substrates.
At the end of the catalytic reaction, characterization of the used MOFs should be made trying to understand the reasons for deactivation and to device reactivation procedures. For the present catalytic systems, one of the most general causes of deactivation is Pd leaching from the solid to the liquid phase. This could be determined by chemical analysis of the liquid phase containing the reaction mixture in each cycle as well as analysis of the Pd content of MOFs in a final reused catalyst. Besides diminution of Pd content on the solid leaching can also cause variations of the initial particle size by Pd dissolution and re-precipitation. Electron microscopy and determination of a particle size should be used to assess the dimension of the Pd NPs before and after its use as catalysts.
In addition to determination of Pd content and average particle size, the reused catalyst should also be surveyed for crystallinity of the host solid. Considering that it is a general fact that many MOFs can collapse under chemical stress, it is necessary to show that the material has not become totally or partially amorphous during its continued use as a catalyst. Also, abrasion due to the stirring of the material has to be checked by scanning electron microscopy to determine variation in the morphology and dimensions of MOF crystallites.
Finally, related with the possibility of Pd leaching is the issue of whether or not the process using MOFs as catalysts is truly heterogeneous. It has been frequently observed that for solid catalysts a small percentage of Pd leaching takes place and this minute concentration is responsible for a large extent of the catalytic process. In this case, the solid acts as a reservoir of Pd providing a constant concentration of Pd in solution. This Pd in the solution is the real active species or at least it may contribute in a significant percentage to the overall catalytic activity. A simple test to evaluate the importance of the contribution of Pd leaching with regard to the product formation is the so called hot-filtration test. In this type of experiment, the reaction is started in the presence of a catalyst until a conversion of about $30\%$ of the maximum value is achieved and then the solid is removed at the reaction temperature and the neat filtrate is submitted to further reaction in the absence of the solid. Under these conditions, if Pd leaching has occurred in the initial stage of the reaction, then further conversion should be observed even in the absence of solid. Occurrence of Pd leaching is always detrimental from the point of catalyst stability since it always leads the depletion of Pd loading on the catalyst and to variations in the particle size distribution. Other alternatives to the hot-filtration test are the use of poisons of homogeneous Pd species $\mathrm{\tilde{H}g\it}$ or thiols) or the three-phases test. In the three-phases test one of the substrate is covalently anchored on a solid different from the one containing the catalyst and then the solid having attached the substrate surveyed for product formation. The three-phases experiments can also be used to assess the heterogeneity of the catalytic reaction.
## General overview of cross-coupling reactions
## Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling
The Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling120,121 between an aryl halide and an arylboronic acid is the most general reaction to form biaryl compounds including those containing heterocyclic moieties. The general reactivity in this transformation follows the order iodides $>$ bromides $>$ chlorides. The presence of electron withdrawing substituents increases the reactivity of aryl halide that decreases by the presence of ortho substituents due to steric hindrance. The reaction requires the presence of bases such as carbonates, phosphates or amines and is generally favoured by polar solvents. Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling has been reported using Pd-containing solids like Pd/hydrotalcite catalysts,122 Pd exchanged on oxides,123 metal oxides and aluminosilicates,124 silica-supported Pd,73 Pd loaded on dealuminated Y zeolites125 and Pd-supported on ordered porous solids.126 However, the catalytic activity of Pd on these inorganic supports is limited in scope and sometimes show poor catalyst stability.
In this context, in a seminal contribution by Corma and co-workers, a Pd-MOF was synthesised on purpose to implement the typical catalytic activity of Pd. This Pd-MOF is structurally related to 3D sodalite-type frameworks, with two different hexagonal windows with free openings of 4.8 and $8.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ and with a fraction of $42\%$ of the crystal volume available to adsorbed guests. $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ as isolated ions is coordinated to the organic linker. The Pd-MOF molecular formula was of $\mathrm{\big[Pd}\mathrm{\big(}2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo)}_{2}\mathrm{\big]}_{n}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and its activity was evaluated in the Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling reaction between phenylboronic acid and 4-bromoanisole reaching $85\%$ conversion and $99\%$ selectivity towards 4-methoxybiphenyl after $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Scheme 1).76 The X-ray diffractograms of used Pd-MOF was similar to that of fresh sample indicating that there is no appreciable loss of crystallinity during its use as a catalyst. The integrity of Pd-MOF was further ascertained by performing reusability tests and chemical analysis of the recovered Pd-MOF. The turnover frequency (TOF) for the Pd-MOF $\left(0.25~\mathrm{mol\%}~\mathrm{P}\right.$ d) catalyst was calculated to be a value of $1230\mathrm{h}^{-1}(20.5\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ . The interest of this Pd-MOF is that it shows that isolated $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ forming the nodes of the crystal structure can participate in a mechanism that involves swing between two oxidation stages (Fig. 3) without destroying the integrity of the framework. Understanding of how the MOF adapts to the changes between $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ requires further studies.
