The structure of MIL-53(Al)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ having uncoordinated amine groups as a substituent of the terephthalate linker defines 1D channels with different pore sizes depending on the nature of the inserted molecules. $\mathbf{\overset{130-132}{M I L}\overline{{-53}}(A l)\ –N H_{2}}$ possesses high thermal stability (up to $400~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and chemical stability to water and common organic solvents. Pd NPs have been deposited on the matrix of $\mathsf{M I L}{-}53(\mathsf{A l}){-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ MOF by a direct ionic exchange followed by reduction.78 The rationale in the use of MIL-53(Al)- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is that the amino groups protruding into the channels should assist Pd NPs stability by interacting with the NPs. Accordingly, the resulting Pd NPs were highly dispersed in $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ with a mean diameter of $3.12~\mathrm{nm}$ at $0.97~\mathrm{wt\%}$ loading. This catalyst was tested for Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling reaction between aryl halides and phenylboronic acid (Scheme 2).78 High yield of biphenyl $(>90\%)$ was observed when iodo- and bromobenzene were used as substrates, however, only trace amount of product was observed with chlorobenzene. For this substrate, addition of sodium methoxide resulted in the formation of the cross-coupling product in $59\%$ yield, but deterioration of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ host was observed. Sterically hindered 2-methoxybromobenzene showed $88\%$ yield of the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling product, indicating the excellent catalytic activity of the $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst. The solid catalyst was used five times for the formation of biphenyl from bromobenzene and phenylboronic acid, observing only a very low concentration of Pd $(0.3\ \mathrm{ppm})$ in the solution as revealed by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) analysis. It is, however, pertinent to comment that it is unclear whether $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Al}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ outperforms or not the previously presented $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-101}$ that promotes the crosscoupling of chlorobenzene including 2-chloroanisole in high yields. It would be certainly important to rank the activity of these two extremely active Pd-containing MOFs.
Pd NPs have been embedded over an amino-functionalized MOF, namely $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101(\mathbf{Cr}){-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2},$ to form $\mathbf{Pd}\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{MIL-101}\mathbf{(Cr)-NH_{2}}$ with approximately 4, 8, 12 and $16~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Pd and their activity tested in the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction in water.79 The reaction between 4-bromoanisole and phenylboronic acid with $\mathbf{Cs_{2}C O_{3}}$ as base using $8\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\substack{-101}(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as a catalyst resulted in quantitative yield of 4-methoxybiphenyl in water at room temperature. Although NaOH, $\mathrm{i}{\cdot}\mathrm{Pr}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ , $\mathrm{i-Pr}_{2}\mathrm{EtN}$ and $\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ also afforded quantitative yield for this reaction, it was found that $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ is not stable in the presence of these bases. Out of the various arylboronic acid derivatives tested for the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling with 4-bromoanisole, pinacol phenylboronate (Scheme 3) afforded the highest yield, followed by phenyltrifluoroborate potassium salt and phenylboronic acid.79 Satisfactory and very similar coupling yields were obtained with 4- and $8\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\substack{-101}\mathrm{(Cr)}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , but 12- and $16~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were less efficient.79 Interestingly, $8~\mathrm{{wt^{0}/o}~P d@M I L\cdot101(C r)\mathrm{{\cdot}N H_{2}}}$ afforded the corresponding biphenyl in 99 and $70\%$ yields, when the coupling with pinacol phenylboronate was performed at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in water using 4-chlorobenzaldehyde and 4-chloromethylbenzoate, respectively, with little or no formation of undesired byproducts. The catalyst, $8\mathrm{\DeltaWt^{0}/o\ P d{@M L101(C r)}{\cdot N H_{2}}}$ exhibited high activity even after 10 cycles for the reaction between 4-bromotoluene and pinacol phenylboronate.79 On the other hand, the powder XRD pattern of the recovered catalyst after the 10th cycle was different from that of $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101(\mathbf{Cr}){\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , but similar to that of another phase, presumably $\mathbf{MIL}–88\mathbf{B}–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ , suggesting that there could have been a transformation of the initial MOF structure into a different one. On the other hand, TEM analysis after the 10th cycle indicated the occurrence of only a limited aggregation of Pd NPs on the surface of the MOF catalyst. Elemental analysis of the recycled $8\mathrm{\:wt^{0}/o\ P d(\widehat{a}M L\cdot101(C r)\cdot N H_{2}}$ material revealed an overall loss of $0.96~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd after 10 runs. These characterization data indicate a certain deterioration of the catalyst upon extensive use, but considerably lower than the activity loss occurring from other solid catalysts.
$\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}L_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]{\cdot4N O_{3}\cdot H_{2}O}}}$ and $[\mathrm{{CuLCl}_{2}]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ MOFs (see Scheme 4 for the structure of L) have been synthesised using an organic linker (L) that can generate easily a N-heterocyclic carbene (NHC) as an anchor in the pores of palladium(II) ions introduced by post-modification.80 As commented in the introduction, Pd–NHCs are among the most stable and efficient Pd complexes to promote a cross-coupling reaction. The ligand was designed in such a way that it contains two charged aromatic azolium components (Scheme 4), the carboxylate groups can bridge metal nodes to form diverse topological MOFs, while the azolium components tether catalytic active metal sites. Formation of a Pd complex in $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}L_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]cdot4N O_{3}{\cdot}H_{2}O}}$ was performed treating this MOF with $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ in THF resulting in about $11\%$ wt Pd immobilized to give Pd– $\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}L_{2}\big(M e O H)_{2}\big]\cdot4N O_{3}\cdot H_{2}O}.$ 80 Furthermore, formation of Pd NPs in Pd– $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}L_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]cdot4N O_{3}{\cdot}H_{2}O}}$ was not observed. XPS revealed the existence of divalent Pd. The catalytic activity of Pd– $\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}L_{2}\big(M e O H)_{2}\big]\cdot4N O_{3}\cdot H_{2}O}$ was investigated in the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction of bromobenzene and phenylboronic acid in toluene at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ in the presence of ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ whereby quantitative yield of biphenyl was observed.80 For comparison, $\mathbf{Pd/C}$ exhibited $8\%$ yield under identical reaction conditions. The powder XRD pattern of the recovered solid showed the structural integrity of the catalyst during the catalytic process, while TEM and XPS measurements confirmed that Pd NPs were not formed and the oxidation state of Pd remained divalent. Although the catalyst showed high activity for aryl iodides and bromides, there was no information about the catalytic activity of Pd– $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}L_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]{\cdot4N O_{3}\cdot H_{2}O}}}$ for aryl chlorides which are challenging substrates, but more interesting due to their lower cost and easier availability.
Metal–organic nanotube (MONT), with the molecular formula of $\mathbf{ZnL^{\prime}C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ has been synthesized by linking up the bent organic ligand, $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -methylenebis(3-(4-carboxy-2-methylphenyl)- $1H\cdot$ -imidazol-3-ium) (see Scheme 5 for $\mathbf{L}^{\prime}$ ) and the tetracoordinated zinc cations.81 The organic linker of $\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{nL^{\prime}C l}_{2}$  ${\mathrm{8H}}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ can be easily deprotonated to anchor palladium ions forming a NHC similar to the previously commented case of $\mathrm{Pd}{\bf-}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}{\bf\cdot}4\mathrm{NO}_{3}{\bf\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{Pd\mathrm{-}C u L C l_{2}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ .80 Adsorption of $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ was considered as evidence that the imidazolium moieties in $\mathbf{ZnL^{\prime}C l}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ are partially deprotonated resulting in the formation of $\mathbf{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ –NHCs. The catalytic activity of solid $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}Z n L^{\prime}C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ was evaluated in the Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling reaction of bromobenzene with phenylboronic acid and resulting in the quantitative yield of biphenyl.81 In comparison, under these reaction conditions, $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Pd/C}$ gave 22 and $1\%$ yields of biphenyl. The catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}Z n L^{\prime}C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ was much superior to that of analogous catalysts with a similar Pd–NHC building unit but lower porosity.80 Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) did not show traces of palladium in the filtrate. These results demonstrate the heterogeneous catalytic behaviour of $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}Z n L^{\prime}C l_{2}\cdot8H_{2}O}$ . The recovered catalyst was subsequently used in six successive runs without observing decay in its catalytic efficiency. Powder XRD pattern of the recovered solid indicated that the structural integrity of the catalyst is maintained during the catalytic process. The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{ZnL}^{\prime}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was not checked using chlorobenzenes, which are more challenging substrate, but competitively more convenient than bromobenzenes due to their cost-effectiveness.
MOF-5 is one of the well studied MOFs, whose structure is constituted by clusters of $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O^{6+}}$ as metal nodes and bdc as an organic linker to form a zeolite-like, cubic framework.11 Pd NPs have been loaded on the matrix of MOF-5 by chemical vapour deposition using $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5})(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5})$ as a precursor, followed by reduction at room temperature to obtain $\mathbf{Pd/MOF{-}}5^{82}$ with Pd NPs size ranging between 2 and $5\mathrm{nm}$ . Powder XRD showed that the crystal structure of MOF-5 was retained even at high Pd loading $\left(4.2~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ .82 The catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{MOF}{-}5$ was studied for the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction of phenylboronic acid and bromobenzene, whereby high conversion, $81\%$ , was attained with $4.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Pd in DMF using $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as base.82 The catalyst with $4.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd NPs was used for second, third and fourth runs, observing that the conversion decreased to 38, 29 and $21\%$ , respectively. Powder XRD indicated the disappearance of characteristic reflections corresponding to MOF-5 even after the first run. The scope of this catalyst with other substrates is lacking. It should be noted that there are in the literature133,134 sufficient data showing that the framework stability of MOF-5 is low and it deteriorates rapidly under mild treatments. Therefore, it seems at first sight that selection of this MOF as a host would raise serious problems of catalyst stability, as it was in fact observed here.
IRMOF-3 is a well known MOF with a cubic topology prepared from $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid.24 The amino group of the ligand does not participate in coordination to the tetranuclear $\mathrm{{zn}_{4}O}$ nodes, thus, allowing post-modification of the IRMOF-3 through a variety of organic transformations.135–137 In this context, IRMOF-3 has been modified by reaction between the amine groups of IRMOF-3 linkers and pyridine-2-aldehyde leading to the formation of a bidentate Schiff base moiety in the porous matrix (Scheme 6), which was used as anchoring sites for $\mathbf{PdCl}_{2}$ .83 The catalytic activity of the resulting IRMOF-3-PI-Pd was studied in the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction. The reaction conditions were optimized with bromobenzene and phenylboronic acid using $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as base in a $20\%$ aqueous–ethanol medium achieving quantitative yield of biphenyl (TOF: $3056\ \mathrm{~h~}^{-1})$ .83 Although high activity was observed using iodobenzene and bromobenzene as reactants, this catalytic system becomes inefficient for chlorobenzene as a substrate by affording only $19\%$ yield of the corresponding product. The size selectivity of the coupling reaction catalyzed using IRMOF-3-PI-Pd was ascertained by performing reaction with an equimolecular mixture of 9-bromoanthracene and bromobenzene, observing that bromobenzene was fully converted to biphenyl, whereas 9-bromoanthrecene remains unreactive. This selectivity was interpreted as indicating that the functionalized pore windows of IRMOF-3-PI-Pd do not allow 9-bromoanthracene to diffuse through the pores owing to its large size. Chemical analysis of the recovered catalysts confirmed that the palladium content of the IRMOF-3-PI-Pd remains identical after its reuse, although, hot filtration experiments are compatible with the presence of palladium in the solution during the reaction. This could be again another example of the so-called ‘‘boomerang’’ effect in heterogeneous Pd catalysis.138 IRMOF-3-PI-Pd was reused for five successive runs without any decay in its activity. The structural integrity of the recovered catalyst was supported by XRD, IR spectroscopy and gas adsorption measurements. Furthermore, TEM of the recovered catalyst showed no sign of formation of Pd NPs on the surface of the catalyst.
Nanometric MOF namely, ScBTC with a pore diameter of $5.7~\times~5.7~\times~3.4~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ was synthesised from scandium(III) and multiple-carboxyl bridging ligand btc by a facile surfactantassisted microwave irradiation method. Pd NPs have been supported on ScBTC using a simple and effective microwaveassisted impregnation process and their catalytic activity was checked in the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction.84 Pd-ScBTC NMOF exhibited high catalytic activity under mild reaction conditions ( $40~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ in air) for both electron-rich parasubstituted aryl bromides and electron-deficient para-substituted arylboronic acids affording the cross-coupling products in excellent yields.84 Interestingly, sterically hindered 2-bromoanisole having a strong electron-donating substituent also gave $87\%$ yield. However, when the less active chlorobenzene was reacted with phenylboronic acid low yield $(22\%)$ was observed even at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ after $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ in air. Interestingly, the yield of biphenyl was remarkably increased to $78\%$ when $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ was replaced by ${\mathrm{NaOCH}}_{3}$ as the base and tetrabutylammonium bromide was added. Notably, 3-chloropyridine was coupled with phenylboronic acid to afford the target product in $90\%$ yield. Comparison of the activity of Pd-ScBTC NMOF with commercial $5\ \mathrm{wt\%}\ \mathrm{Pd/C}$ catalyst ( $\mathrm{1~mol\%}$ Pd) and bulk ScBTC MOF-supported Pd catalyst (Pd-ScBTC MOFs, $3.2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , $1\mathrm{\mol{\%}}$ Pd) for the coupling of chlorobenzene and phenylboronic acid in the different solvents showed the order as $\mathbf{\mathrm{Pd}}/\mathbf{C}~<~\mathbf{\mathrm{Pd}}{\cdot}\mathbf{MOFs}$ $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathbf{\times}\mathbf{\mathrm{1oFs}}<$ Pd-NMOFs. Heterogeneity test showed less than 1 ppm of Pd in the reaction mixtures. The catalyst was reused five times without decrease in activity for iodobenzene and phenylboronic acid coupling. Reusability should also be checked for less reactive substrates.
A different post-functionalization route has been reported for the inclusion of oxamate units as a chelating agent.85 In a first post-synthesis step, reduction of $\mathbf{NO}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Cr})$ resulted in the formation of $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathbf{Cr})$ . The second step was the condensation of the amino groups of the ligand with ethyl chloro-oxoacetate resulting in the formation of oxamates attached to the linker. Highly dispersed Pd NPs were incorporated to obtain Pd/PMA-MIL-101(Cr) (Scheme 7).
XPS analysis showed that both, metallic and cationic Pd species are present in the material. When lower precursor concentrations were used, moderate Pd loadings $(6~\mathrm{wt\%})$ were obtained. In this case, TEM images did not show the formation of large aggregates, but well dispersed and small palladium NPs $\left(2{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ were formed. The catalytic activity of this solid was evaluated in Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling of bromobenzene and phenylboronic acid, using DMF as solvent in the presence of $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .85 Around $50\%$ conversion of bromobenzene was achieved with TOF values of $10\mathrm{~h~}^{-1}$ . A very similar catalytic performance was observed for the first 4 uses. In contrast, after the fourth run hot filtration test showed a similar catalytic profile for a twin experiment without removing the solid, suggesting Pd leaching. Low Pd concentrations, in the order of ppm, were demonstrated as catalytically active sites. This raises the issue about whether or not the reaction is really heterogeneous or the solid is acting as a reservoir of Pd providing a low, but steady, concentration of Pd in to the solution, similarly to what we have commented earlier in the boomerang effect.
Cohen and co-workers have reported the synthesis of a highly crystalline $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{\Big(r\big)}.$ -based MOFs, namely UiO-67-bpydc containing open $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine chelating sites which readily form complexes with $\mathbf{PdCl}_{2}$ to result in a MOF that shows efficient and recyclable catalytic activity for the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction between phenylboronic acid and 4-bromotoluene to 4-phenyltoluene in $89\%$ yield at $95^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{C}$ (Scheme 8).86 In contrast, pristine UiO-67 and UiO-67-bpyd $\mathbf{\hat{s}}_{0.5}/\mathrm{bpdc}_{0.5}$ resulted in no conversion. Under the same reaction conditions, $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ and $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ gave 54 and $51\%$ yields. Furthermore, commercial $10\%$ Pd/C catalyst exhibited $63\%$ yield. The recovered catalyst was used in successive runs without decrease in the yield $\left(>85\%\right)$ in three reactions. Further, powder XRD and SEM of UiO-67-Pdbpydc $_{0.5}/$ $\mathrm{\bpdc_{0.5}}$ after catalysis showed that the MOF was still highly crystalline in agreement with the robust nature of the UiO-67 catalyst. However, the study was limited to only few substrates and the scope of this catalyst, particularly with respect to the activity to promote coupling of aryl chlorides, still remains to be fully substantiated.
UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ is constituted by zirconium–oxo clusters with 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylates with uncoordinated amine groups, defining a three dimensional structure. The amino groups in $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are available for post-synthetic modification.139 UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ contains highly stable secondary building units comparable to the parent UiO-66 material, which leads to an inimitable thermal stability (decomposition temperature is above $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and a strong resistance to many chemicals such as water, benzene and ethanol. $\mathsf{\Pi}^{140}\mathsf{P d/U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}}$ has been prepared and its catalytic activity in Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction studied.87 The reaction between phenylboronic acid and 4-iodoanisole (Scheme 9) with $0.25\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ exhibited $95\%$ yield of the product with ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ as the base in $\mathrm{DMF/H_{2}O}$ as solvent at $60\ {}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ 87 This catalytic system showed a wide substrate scope for the synthesis of various biphenyls starting from substrates including aryl chlorides, requiring however, a higher temperature $(80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ . Interestingly, the TOF achieved for the reaction between phenyliodide with phenylboronic acid using $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Al}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ,7 $\mathrm{^{3}}\mathrm{\bf\DeltaPd/MIL-}53(\mathrm{Al}),\mathrm{^{78}}$ IRMOF-3-PI-Pd83 and $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH_{2}}^{87}$ was 396, 98, 2037 and $2190.5\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ could be reused for five consecutive cycles with a slight decrease in the yield. XRD of the recovered catalyst indicated that the structure of $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is not altered after five cycles. The amount of Pd leaching determined by ICP analysis shows that only small amounts of Pd (no data were given) are leached in the first two runs.
Uncoordinated amino groups have been frequently used as anchoring sites for metal cations or metal NPs. It can be speculated that the uncoordinated oxygen atoms (with lone pair electrons) in MOFs could also act as anchoring sites for metal cations through coordination interactions.141,142 In this aspect, porous Co(II)-salicylate (MCoS-1) was synthesised from cobalt(II) chloride hexahydrate and salicylic acid. Pd NPs have been grafted on the surface of MCoS-1 (Pd/MCoS-1) through the uncoordinated oxygen atoms and its catalytic activity tested in the Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling in water leading to the synthesis of a diverse range of biaryls.88 The Pd loading was $1\mathrm{wt\%}$ and TEM images showed the existence of Pd nanospheres of $5{\mathrm{-}}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ dimension uniformly distributed throughout the sample. 4-Bromobenzaldehyde and phenylboronic acid reacted at $70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ in air using ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ as base and water as the reaction medium to yield $96\%$ of 4-carbaldehydebiphenyl (Scheme 10).88 Interestingly, less reactive chlorobenzene showed $60\%$ yield of biphenyl. However, the activated aryl chloride, 4-acetylchlorobenzene, gave the corresponding product in $85\%$ yield using Pd/MCoS-1. Sensitive heteroaryl halides bearing a pyridyl moiety produced the corresponding aryl–heteroaryl coupling product in $88\%$ yield. Sterically hindered 2-nitrobromobenzene also showed $86\%$ of the coupling product. AAS analysis revealed the absence of Pd ions in the liquid phase. The catalyst was reused up to six cycles with only a marginal decrease in activity. HRTEM analysis of the reused Pd/MCoS-1 catalyst after two Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reactions showed that the size of the Pd NPs is even reduced to $2{\cdot}3~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and that they are more finely dispersed.
MOF-253 was prepared from the hydrothermal reaction of $\mathbf{AlCl}_{3}$ and bpydc acid with the molecular formula of Al(OH)(bpydc). Pd(II) was immobilized on MOF-253 by post-synthetic modification. In the MOF structure, the bpy moieties were not coordinated with any metal ions, providing consequently a platform for the chelation of Pd. Various $\mathbf{MOF}–253{\cdot}x\mathbf{P}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}_{2}$ ( $(x,$ molar ratio of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ to bpydc) were prepared. XPS revealed the existence of $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ cations. Substituted aryl iodides, with either electronwithdrawing or electron-donating groups were tested as substrates for the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reactions, with phenylboronic acid reaching quantitative yields of the corresponding products for all these substrates.89 Importantly, MOF$253{\cdot}0.05\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ also catalyzed the coupling of aryl chlorides effectively, although higher catalyst loadings, stronger base like KOH, and longer reaction times were needed.89 Substituted aryl chlorides with either electron-withdrawing groups (–COMe and $\left.-\mathbf{NO}_{2}\right.$ ) or moderately electron-donating groups $\left(-\mathbf{CH}_{3}\right)$ afforded the corresponding cross-coupling products in excellent yields (Scheme 11). However, no data were reported about catalyst stability and reusability and this relevant point still needs to be studied.
A $\mathbf{Pd/Y}$ heterobimetallic MOFs (Pd/Y-MOF) was synthesized by coordination of $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathbf{Y}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ with bpydc under microwave irradiation.90 Formation of 3D extended framework resulted from linking $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ building blocks via $\mathbf{Y}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ coordinating to carboxylic groups. The powder XRD pattern displays a long-range crystalline structure. A series of aryl halides and 4-methylphenylboronic acid were employed as reactants in Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling reaction to evaluate the catalytic performance of Pd/Y-MOF.90 It was observed, however, that the catalytic activity decreased from iodobenzene to bromobenzene with the respective yields of 96 and $59\%$ that is in accordance with enhanced difficulty for activating the C–halide bond. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{\bfPd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{\bfCl_{2}}$ catalyst gave only $65\%$ yield with iodobenzene which is much lower than the activity achieved with Pd/Y-MOF. Pd/Y-MOF as a heterogeneous catalyst showed an identical catalytic activity as of $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ as a homogeneous catalyst in the reaction between 4-methylphenylboronic acid and iodobenzene. The fact that Pd/Y-MOF performs as ${\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ indicates the presence of highly dispersed $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ active sites and the absence of diffusion limitations. However, different activity between $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ and Pd/Y-MOF was clearly seen for those molecules having larger dimension. The influence of substrate dimension on the catalytic activity of a porous solid has frequently been attributed to the confinement of the active sites inside the pores, meaning that the reaction takes place predominantly within the internal voids of the solid. Thus, depending on the molecular size, diffusion limitations experienced by larger iodobenzenes to reach the active sites in Pd/YMOF decrease the reaction rate whereas for homogeneous catalysts, the reaction still shows high activity due to easy accessibility of substrates to the active sites. With respect to scope of the catalyst and its activity rank, it was found that the coupling reaction between chlorobenzene and 4-methylphenylboronic acid reached low conversion $(22\%)$ even after longer reaction times in the presence of tetrabutylammonium bromide. Pd/Y-MOF catalyst was reused five times for the reaction between iodobenzene and 4-methylphenylboronic acid with slight decrease in its activity. A series of tests including hot filtration, solid-phase poisoning test and three-phase test indicated that the catalysis is heterogeneous in nature.
Uozumi and co-workers have designed a metal–network complex of a $C_{3}$ -trisphosphine with flexible alkyl-chain tethers (Scheme 12) with Pd species through Pd–P coordination.91 Although this type of flexible alkyl chains has not been used for the preparation of insoluble 3D metal–network complexes, hydrophobic alkyl chains are critical in forming a pliable network to capture organic substrates efficiently in the matrix of a 3D Pd complex. The catalytic activity of this complex was evaluated in the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling between iodobenzene and phenylboronic acid achieving quantitative yield in water.91 The catalyst was reused four times with gradual decrease in its yield. However, the activity is limited only to iodo/bromobenzenes and data about Pd leaching is not provided.
In another precedent, a self-supported palladium catalyst was prepared via construction of a MOF of a bipyridyl-palladium complex bearing carboxylic groups and a copper(II) linker ( $\mathrm{\PdCl}_{2}$ bpy–Cu, Scheme 13).92 Although $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ bpy–Cu efficiently catalyzed the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling of iodo/bromobenzenes with 4-methylphenylboronic acid in $90\%$ yields in water, an attempt to use chlorobenzene as aryl halide results in only $2\%$ yield. $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ bpy–Cu was reused for five runs with no significant change in the yield. However, TEM image of the recovered catalyst after the reaction showed that the catalyst is not stable with the appearance of big particles $\sim100\ \mathrm{nm})$ with individual Pd NPs of $5\mathrm{nm}$ within the block. No data were found on the leaching of Pd under these experimental conditions.
Based on hard-soft acid–base theory, the possibility of combining a soft nitrophilic metal cation to preferentially coordinate with diimine groups of a mixed donor ligand such as bpydc, leading to a metalloligand with the harder carboxylate groups available to bind with other oxophilic metals, can be visualized. In this context, a new strategy has been reported for the generation of metalloligand $\mathrm{\big[Pd\big(H_{2}b p y d c\big)C l_{2}\big]}$ with the ideal catalytic site being defined by the $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{diimine})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ unit. Further, subsequent complexation of carboxylate groups by $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ $[\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Sm}$ , Eu, Gd or Tb) resulted in two kinds of functional metal centers $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ as linker-bridging metal centre and $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ as catalytic metal center) constituting isomorphous porous materials with the formula of $\{\mathrm{Ln}_{2}[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{Hbpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}-(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\}_{n}$ (Scheme 14, $\mathbf{L}\mathbf{n}=\mathbf{S}\mathbf{m}$ , Eu, Gd, and Tb).93 The catalytic activity of these MOFs was studied in Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling. The reaction of phenylboronic acid with bromobenzene and iodobenzene afforded 93 and $97\%$ yields, respectively, using $\{\mathrm{Sm}_{2}[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{Hbpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}-(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\}_{n}$ as catalyst with $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as base. Comparing with the high yield achieved from bromo/iodobenzenes, the use of chlorobenzene results only $5\%$ yield under the same conditions. Furthermore, comparable yields were observed with the other Ln MOF catalysts. No data were reported on its reusability and characterization of the spent catalyst. In contrast, no Pd leaching was detected by AAS analysis.
As commented earlier, catalytically active sites like $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ have been introduced onto the pores of MOFs by direct incorporation methods. Recently, a new strategy was developed using a mixed ligand MOF with isolated Pd single active sites in the MOF network in a uniform way.94 The use of a high proportion of bpdc ligands $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2},\ 90\ \mathrm{mol}\%\right)$ that cannot chelate the Pd complex was essential to obtain low Pd concentration. This low concentration of Pd ensures the separation and dispersion of the metalloligand $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{1}$ and the presence of single Pd cations in the individual cages of the MOF architecture (Scheme 15). This site isolation should prevent mutual deactivation between sites that are too close together. This rational design of MOFs is expected to exhibit higher activity due to the low Pd content with a maximum exposure of the active sites to reactants. In this context, $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ was immobilized on a porous UiO-67 $\left(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\ @\mathrm{UiO}{-}67\right)$ using a direct incorporation strategy.94 The Pd content was estimated to be $1.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , with Pd NPs being not detectable.
The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\textcircled{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ was screened using 4-nitro-1-chlorobenzene and phenylboronic acid as substrates and observing $97\%$ isolated yield in DMF–EtOH mixture and ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ as a base.94 On the other hand, the parent UiO-67 MOF, one of the components of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\textcircled{\mathrm{2}}\mathrm{UiO-}67$ like $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{1}$ and homogeneous complexes namely $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{2}$ exhibited 0, 50, 0 and $3\%$ yields, respectively, of the corresponding coupling product. Notably, 2-chloroanisole which is both deactivated and sterically hindered gave the corresponding coupling product in $80\%$ yield. Furthermore, 2-chloropyridine and 2-chlorothiophene with phenylboronic acid (Scheme 16) afforded the corresponding coupling products in 90 and $88\%$ yields, respectively. These data further confirm the importance of highly dispersed single-site $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ immobilized on MOFs in achieving a high activity in this coupling reaction. This is one of the efficient catalysts reported so far for the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling using chlorobenzene. It would be also interesting to compare the activity of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\textcircled{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ with bromo/iodobenzene in order to delineate efficiency of this process.
Recently, Ebitani and co-workers have designed an amino functionalized organozinc coordination polymer (AZC) for efficient grafting of Pd species leading to highly dispersed and easily accessible active centers $\big(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}/\mathrm{AZC}\big)$ .95 A remarkable turnover number (TON) of 2106720 was achieved for the reaction of bromobenzene and phenylboronic acid $599\%$ yield) under the optimized conditions.95 Also, the catalyst showed no significant loss of activity up to seven runs. In contrast, the reaction between chlorobenzene and phenylboronic acid afforded $18\%$ yield of biphenyl in the presence of tetrabutylammonium bromide.
## Mizoroki–Heck coupling
The Mizoroki–Heck coupling between aryl halides and alkenes catalyzed by Pd complexes and salts is a useful reaction to form $\upbeta$ -substituted styrenes.143,144 MIL-101 is one of the most stable and robust MOFs that has found large applications in heterogeneous catalysis.96 It is possible to submit pristine MIL-101 to post-synthetic modification employing strategies based on the anchoring onto the free position around the metal (Cr or Fe) sites. Specifically, diamino groups can anchor strongly one amine group to the framework metal while the other amino group is free to interact with other different metal ion hosted in the pore of the solid. Following this strategy, two different MIL-101 catalysts, namely ED-MIL-101 (Scheme 17; ED: ethylenediamine) and APSMIL-101 (APS: 3-aminopropyltrialkoxysilane), have been prepared by coordination to the dehydrated MIL-101 framework.
MIL-101 does not undergo apparent loss of crystallinity upon ED functionalization, observing only some slight variations of the Bragg intensities of the material. Palladium loaded APS-MIL-101 and ED-MIL-101 have shown comparable activity to that of $1\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ as a catalyst for the Mizoroki–Heck reaction between iodobenzene and acrylic acid in $_{N,N}$ -dimethylacetamide (Scheme 18).96 The reusability test showed the absence of Pd leaching. Considering that iodobenzene is the most reactive aryl halide for $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}$ cross-coupling reactions and in order to check the catalytic activity of Pd NPs loaded on amino modified MIL-101, it would be of interest to expand the scope of this Pd catalyst to other more challenging couplings and explore the use of this catalyst for other palladium catalyzed reactions. The Pd amine-grafted MIL-101 as a catalyst exhibited a short induction period of 0.5 to 1 h that has been proposed to be due to the slow diffusion of reactants. However, if this was the case, similar induction periods should also be observed for other reactions involving MOFs. Considering the small size of reactants, further experiments may clarify this issue.
MIL-53(Al) $\left[{\bf A l(O H)(b d c)}\right]$ is one of the well exploited materials that has been extensively used in gas storage and separation science.145,146 On the other hand, amine-functionalized MIL${53}(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is an analogue of MIL-53(Al), but it contains free amine groups.130,132,147 Recently, Baiker and co-workers have reported that amine-functionalized mix-linker MIL-53(Al) materials exhibit higher thermal stability than $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ .148 These free amine groups drastically enhance the affinity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and can be used for post-modification with a set of cyclic anhydrides.131 Thus, Cao and co-workers have supported Pd NPs $\left(3.2{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ on amine-functionalized mixed-linker MOFs based on MIL-53(Al) using the ion-exchange method.97 XPS revealed no obvious peak corresponding to $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ and the binding energy value corresponds to Pd in the reduced form. The activity of $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Al}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was checked for the Mizoroki–Heck reaction of bromobenzene and styrene (Scheme 19). A maximum yield of $81\%$ (TOF $270\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ) was achieved with $0.05\mathrm{mol\%}$ of the $\mathbf{Pd/ML}$ ${53}(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ catalyst in DMF using $\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ as a base. The reaction also proceeded with $0.001\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of catalyst giving under these conditions a maximum TOF value of $983~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Blank controls showed no activity for $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The superiority of $\mathrm{Pd}/$ $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ was illustrated comparing its activity with $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ that exhibited only $49\%$ yield with the TOF value of $164\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The Mizoroki–Heck reaction of styrene with iodobenzene proceeded facilely resulting in $t{\cdot}$ -stilbene in $97\%$ yield. As a benchmark to calibrate catalyst activity, the electron-donating and sterically hindered 2-methoxybromobenzene gave $66\%$ yield of the coupling product with styrene. In contrast, the most challenging and readily available chlorobenzene showed only $11\%$ yield with a TOF value of $9\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ after much longer time. ICP analysis showed that the amount of Pd leached into the reaction mixture was very low $(0.1\mathrm{ppm})$ . The reusability data showed that the catalyst can be recycled for five runs with some decline in the yield from 81 to $76\%$ from $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ to $5^{\mathrm{th}}$ run, respectively. The TEM image of the reused catalyst indicated that the mean diameter of the NPs is $3.4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is very similar to the fresh catalyst. All together, the catalytic data indicate that $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Al}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has a high activity to promote the Mizoroki–Heck reaction as a heterogeneous catalyst with a gradual deactivation upon use.
As commented earlier, Pd–NHC type of complexes are among the most stable and active Pd complexes to promote cross-coupling reactions. In a certain way $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}Z n L^{\prime}C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ is the solid equivalent to those soluble Pd–NHC complexes, but having them grafted in the framework. In this aspect, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd-ZnL^{\prime}C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ was tested for the Mizoroki– Heck reaction between 4-bromobenzaldehyde and styrene to obtain the corresponding substituted olefin in quantitative yield in the presence of ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ (Scheme 20).81 A series of aryl halides and olefins were reacted using $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}Z n L^{\prime}C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ as a catalyst to achieve quantitative yields of the substituted olefins. The recovered solid $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}Z n L^{\prime}C l_{2}\cdot8H_{2}O}$ also showed very high activity even after six successive runs.
$\mathbf{MIL}{-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ has been modified in a two-step post-synthetic reaction using maleic anhydride and palladium acetate to obtain MIL-53-(Al)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ –Mal–Pd and the resulting Pd-containing MOFs were used as catalysts in Mizoroki–Heck reactions (Scheme 21).98 XRD patterns of the post-modified samples showed that the structure of $\mathbf{MIL}{-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ is retained throughout the modification process. AAS analysis revealed that the Pd content is $2.1{-}2.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ independent of the amount of precursor.
The catalytic activity of MIL-53-(Al)- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ –Mal–Pd was evaluated in the reaction of bromobenzene with styrene in $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -methylpyrrolidine and sodium acetate as a base to afford full conversion and a selectivity of $90\%$ (Scheme 19). Attempts to use chlorobenzene instead of bromobenzene failed, however, under modified conditions, $21\%$ conversion with $17\%$ yield of $t{\cdot}$ stilbene was observed. MIL-53-(Al)- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}.$ –Mal–Pd was reused three times in the reaction between bromobenzene and styrene, observing that the conversions of the second and third run were significantly lower $(22\%)$ than that of the first run (about $80\%$ ). It was proposed that this decay in activity was due to pore blocking by sodium acetate not completely dissolved in the reaction mixture. If this was the case, washings would be sufficient to recover the activity of the fresh sample. Further studies are required to identify the reasons for the lower stability of this catalytic system. Also, characterization of the spent catalyst could give valuable information on the structural integrity of the catalyst or active sites.
The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{\{Sm_{2}[P d(b p y d c)C l_{2}]_{2}[P d(H b p y d c)C l_{2}]_{2}}.}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}\}_{n}$ has also been investigated in the Mizoroki–Heck reaction between bromobenzene and styrene to achieve $93\%$ yield in DMF.93 A series of coupling products were prepared in high yields using bromobenzene (or iodobenzene) with various olefins. As commented in Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling, using $\{\mathrm{Sm}_{2}[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{Hbpydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}-(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\}_{n}$ as the catalyst the reaction of styrene with chlorobenzene afforded $<5\%$ yield under identical conditions. These catalytic data clearly show that a more efficient catalyst is needed to use chlorobenzene in the coupling reaction. Reusability tests and evidences for the catalyst stability are lacking.
The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd^{2+}}\ @\mathrm{UiO-}67$ was also investigated in the Mizoroki–Heck reaction of 4-chloroacetophenone and styrene (Scheme 22), observing a maximum of $99\%$ conversion of 4-chloroacetophenone with ${>}99\%$ selectivity to the respective coupling product in DMF using tetrabutylammonium bromide and $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ .94 The low yield of $34\%$ achieved by $\mathrm{PdCl_{2}(C H_{3}C N)_{2}}$ as a homogeneous Pd complex under the optimized conditions suggests that the Pd atoms are successfully grafted on the bpy moieties of MOF rather than simply adsorbed on the solid surface. Interestingly, the reaction of 2-chloropyridine with styrene resulted in the corresponding coupling product in $90\%$ yield. The catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\textcircled{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ is found to be superior to that of less porous catalysts with a similar building unit. For example, the reaction of chlorobenzene and styrene in the presence of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\textcircled{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ produced the trans-1,2-diphenylethylene in $81\%$ yield, while the catalyst $\mathrm{(Sm_{2}[P d(b p y d c)C l_{2}]_{2}[P d(H b p y d c)C l_{2}]_{2}}$ gave a negligible coupling product $(>5\%$ yield).93 The low activity of the latter may be attributed to the framework structure that would restrain their catalytic activity significantly. This is one of the most efficient catalytic systems which have used exclusively aryl halides as substrates. $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\textcircled{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ was reused up to five runs with no significant change in its yield. Powder XRD patterns of the used catalyst showed that the structural integrity of the MOF material was mostly retained after the catalytic reactions. Furthermore, XPS of the recovered solid confirmed that the palladium remains in its divalent form after the reaction. Hot filtration test showed 46 ppb of Pd in the reaction mixture.
Recently, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}/\mathrm{AZC}$ was checked in the Mizoroki–Heck reaction of bromobenzene and styrene and achieving $84\%$ yield after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .95
## Sonogashira coupling
One of the important $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ coupling reactions is the Sonogashira reaction between an aryl halide with a terminal alkyne to form the aryl substituted alkyne149 that are intermediates in the synthesis of pharmaceutically relevant compounds.150,151 Pd NPs embedded on MOF-5 $\left(3{-}6\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ were prepared by a chemical deposition method.99 Powder XRD and SEM images of Pd/MOF-5 indicated that the crystal structure is preserved after loading of Pd NPs. The catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{MOF}–5$ was tested in the Sonogashira coupling between iodobenzene and phenylacetylene (Scheme $23)^{99}$ with 1.5 equivalents of $\mathrm{K}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to achieve $98\%$ yield of diphenylacetylene. Pd/MOF-5 also exhibited good activity for many derivatives with various substituents of iodobenzene and phenylacetylene giving the corresponding diarylethynes in good yields $(<90\%)$ . In addition, Pd/MOF-5 showed high activity for aliphatic alkynes and aryl iodide. However, $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{MOF}–5$ as a solid catalyst possesses some limitations. In particular, orthosubstituted iodobenzenes showed lower yields than the para isomer. On the other hand, changing the aryl halide to bromo or chloro resulted in no conversion. Heterogeneity tests showed 2.1 ppm of Pd in the liquid phase after the reaction, while the reusability data indicate that the catalyst loses its activity in the third use. Based on XPS this deactivation was attributed to the oxidation of $\mathrm{Pd}^{0}$ to $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , an oxidation state that was absent in the fresh catalyst. In addition, TEM images showed agglomeration of Pd NPs. Considering that the low structural stability of MOF-5 is by now sufficiently documented, it seems that the selection of this structure to host Pd NPs is, probably, not the best option.
Pd/MCoS-1 has also been tested for the Sonogashira coupling between iodoarene observing phenylacetylene in water under copper-free and phosphine-free conditions and $94\%$ yield of the corresponding product using $\mathrm{NEt}_{3}$ as base.88 To screen the scope of Pd/MCoS-1 MOF as a catalyst, a series of substituted iodo- and bromoarenes were studied for the Sonogashira coupling with yields ranging between $70–95\%$ .88 Chlorobenzene can react with phenylacetylene using 4 equivalent of $\mathrm{NEt}_{3}$ , $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd loading, leading to $44\%$ yield of the 1,2-diphenylacetylene. Heterogeneity of the process and the lack of activity of dissolved Pd species was demonstrated by the lack of influence of the presence of poly(4-vinylpyridine) as a solid poison of solubilised Pd. The catalyst was reused up to six cycles observing only a marginal decrease in the activity.
Considering the activity of the Pd/Y-MOF catalyst in the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling, it was also of interest to determine the activity of Pd/Y-MOF to promote Sonogashira coupling.90 The reaction between phenylacetylene and iodobenzene resulted in $92\%$ yield using Pd/Y-MOF as a catalyst. In contrast, the $\mathrm{\bfPd}(\mathrm{bpydc})\mathrm{\bfCl_{2}}$ homogeneous catalyst gave $61\%$ yield under the same conditions. It was proposed that the enhanced activity of the Pd/Y-MOF catalyst was due to the cooperation between $\mathbf{Y}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ and $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ . The catalyst was reused for three runs with negligible yield decrease. However, the reaction of 4-t-Bu-iodobenzene with phenylacetylene in the presence of $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{Y}/\mathbf{MOF}$ gave only $7.5\%$ yield. This experiment supports that the reaction is taking place within the pores of the Pd/Y-MOF and diffusion of bulky molecule to the sites limits its reactivity by operation of shape selectivity.
A facile method for the synthesis of mesoporous coordination polymer Pd-CP1 (CP: coordination polymer) by immobilizing the palladium bis(phosphine) complex (Scheme 24) has been reported and the catalytic activity of the resulting Pd-CP1 investigated in the Sonogashira coupling reaction.100 The morphology of Pd-CP1 is composed of relatively uniform particles with a size ranging from 0.5 to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Furthermore, the powder XRD pattern indicates the presence of mesopores. TEM images of the Pd-CP1 did not show the presence of any small Pd particles embedded within the material. Further, XPS reveals exclusively $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ coordinated with $\mathrm{PPh}_{2}$ -ligands. The Sonogashira cross-coupling was performed between iodobenzene and phenylacetylene in the presence of a catalyst using diisopropylamine as base at room temperature. It was observed that the catalytic activity of the fresh Pd-CP1 was $62\%$ while its corresponding homogeneous catalyst [P $\mathsf{d}{\cdot}(\mathsf{P P h}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}]$ exhibited $89\%$ yield. To reach the same yield Pd-CP1 required double reaction time. These catalytic data clearly indicate that immobilization of the Pd complex slows down the activity of Pd complex, requiring longer time to reach the same yield. However, the heterogeneous catalyst can be recovered and reused, observing that as the recycling steps increases, the activity gradually decreases, being only $43\%$ at the end of the fourth use. No Pd trace was detected in the filtered liquid phase and the observed decrease in the catalytic activity still remains to be understood. Characterization of the solid after the reusability experiments would give valuable information on the deactivation of catalysts. The scope of this catalyst is also highly limited.
In an another precedent, a 2D pillared-bilayer flexible open MOF $\{[\mathbf{Cu(tdc)(bpe)}]_{n}{\cdot}2n(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}){\cdot}n(\mathbf{MeOH})\}$ has been synthesized and its catalytic activity tested in the homocoupling of phenylacetylene.101 The reaction containing phenylacetylene, ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ and $5\ \mathrm{mol\%}$ of a catalyst in toluene at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ atmosphere resulted in $82\%$ yield, while the blank control under identical conditions using $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ gave only $9\%$ yield. This result suggested that the presence of electron rich ligands tdc and bpe might facilitate the in situ reduction of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ to the $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ state which is the presumed responsible species for the coupling. However, this proposal could not be supported experimentally since all attempts to detect $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{I})}}$ ions by EPR and UV-Vis spectroscopy failed. Although identification of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ was not possible, the requirement of $\mathbf{O}_{2}/\mathrm{air}$ for the reaction suggests that $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ probably re-oxidizes the in situ formed $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{I})}}$ ions to regenerate the catalyst. The catalyst was reused for four cycles with a gradual decrease in product yield from the $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ run and at the end of $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ run, only $40\%$ yield was achieved. However, heterogeneity test showed the absence of copper in the solution. Further, powder XRD pattern and FT-IR spectrum of the recovered catalyst suggested that its structure is well maintained after several reactions.
Recently, a crystalline porous MOF, namely, $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(b d c)_{2}(D A B C O)}$ has been synthesized by a solvothermal method using the reaction of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and bdc in DMF and its activity tested in the coupling between phenylboronic acid and phenylacetylene in the presence of molecular oxygen in air as the stoichiometric oxidant (Scheme 25).102 This reaction resulted in $86\%$ conversion using $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ as a catalyst and DBU and bpy as additives in DMF. $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ exhibited significantly higher catalytic activity in this oxidative coupling than that of $\mathbf{Ni}_{3}(\mathbf{btc})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{Ni}(\ b t c)(\ b p y)}$ that convert 51 and $59\%$ of phenylacetylene, respectively. On the other hand, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ were found to be completely inactive for this transformation, while the reaction using $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ as a catalyst led to $72\%$ conversion along with $10\%$ phenylboronic acid homocoupling. Heterogeneity test revealed that the solution does not contain catalytically active species. On the other hand, the catalyst was reused up to four runs without any decrease in the conversion while, it decreased in the fifth and sixth runs. The reasons of this deactivation need to be understood. Although this catalytic system showed wide substrate scope with moderate yields, the need of DBU and bpy as additives to promote this reaction remains yet to be disclosed. The FT-IR spectrum of the reused $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(b d c)_{2}(D A B C O)}$ exhibited no changes compared to the fresh Ni-MOF. Although, the powder XRD of the reused Ni-MOF was slightly different from that of the fresh catalyst, the reused $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(b d c)_{2}(D A B C O)}$ was still highly crystalline.
As commented earlier, one of the well explored methodologies for the anchoring of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions over MOFs is through the linker acting as a ligand of an immobilized complex. In this context, the heterogenization of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions on $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOF-bpy $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b p y d c)_{6}]}}$ to obtain Zr-MOF-bpy:Pd through uncoordinated bpy moieties present in the MOF framework has been reported.103 Zr-MOF-bpy is iso-structural to UiO-67 $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b p d c)_{6}]}$ . XPS revealed the existence of divalent Pd. The catalytic activity of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOF-bpy:Pd was evaluated in the carbonylative Sonogashira coupling between 4-methoxyiodobenzene and phenylacetylene with atmospheric CO pressure (Scheme 26).103 The quantitative product yield was observed when the reaction was performed in the presence of Zr-MOF-bpy:Pd using $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as base in DMF. Interestingly, 1-iodonaphthalene resulted in $94\%$ yield. In contrast, the use of aryl bromides such as bromobenzene, 4-bromobenzaldehyde, 4-bromoanisole, and 4-bromotoluene failed to render the expected coupling products. It is important to develop catalytic systems which can promote couplings using aryl chloride as a reagent to make the entire process cost-effective. The $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFbpy:Pd catalyst was reused for five runs without apparent loss of activity and yield. Furthermore, AAS analysis showed only traces of Pd (less than $0.2\%$ of the total Pd content) in the solution collected by hot filtration after reaction. Powder XRD of the catalyst after the fifth run showed no apparent changes in the crystallinity of the fresh material and TEM showed the absence of Pd aggregation.
Recently, Sonogashira cross-coupling reaction of iodobenzene and phenylacetylene was tested to afford diphenylacetylene in $76\%$ yield using DABCO based $\mathrm{Cu(\mathrm{_I})\mathrm{-}M O F}$ under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere.104 Reused catalysts exhibited consistent activity even after three cycles.
## Ullmann coupling
The Ullmann reaction152 is a homocoupling of aryl halides in the presence of copper based catalysts, leading to the formation of biaryls. In this context, the catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-101}$ was tested for the Ullmann self-coupling of 4-chloroanisole, whereby formation of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dimethoxybiphenyl in $97\%$ yield is observed (Scheme 27).77 Stability of the catalyst was studied by reusing the same sample five times without change in the conversion. In addition, XRD showed that the crystallinity was maintained after its use. With regard to Pd leaching is concerned, less than $0.2\%$ of Pd present in the solid was observed in the solution after the reaction. Finally, TEM showed that the particle size during the coupling reaction is maintained. Thus, all the data point to the stability of Pd/MIL-101 for the Ullmann coupling.
The catalytic activity of $\mathbf{MOF{-}253{\cdot}0.059}\mathbf{{dCl}_{2}}$ was checked in the Ullmann reaction using iodobenzene as a model substrate (Scheme 28).89 Quantitative yield of biphenyl was achieved when the reaction was performed with KOAc as a base, in DMSO:EtOH medium. When the same amount of $\mathbf{Pd}(\log)\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ was used instead of $\mathrm{\sfMOF{-}253{\cdot}0.059\mathrm{dCl}_{2}}$ the product yield was reduced to $50\%$ . The superior activity of $\mathbf{MOF{-}253{\cdot}0.05}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{dCl}_{2}$ compared to its homogeneous counterpart could be related to the presence of adjacent ligands and metal ions in the MOF. Furthermore, when $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ was employed as a catalyst for the same reaction under identical conditions only $10\%$ biphenyl yield was achieved. These catalytic data indirectly support that Pd ions are successfully grafted on the bpy moieties in MOF-253 rather than simply adsorbed on the solid surface. Furthermore, the sterically hindered 2-iodotoluene and 2-iodoanisole also afforded the corresponding coupling products in good yields. A very low amount of dissolved palladium ${<}0.1\%$ of Pd in the MOF catalyst) was detected in the reaction solution after the reaction. The $\mathbf{MOF{-}253{\cdot}0.05}\mathbf{PdCl}_{2}$ was recovered and reused five times without any significant loss of catalytic efficiency. Powder XRD patterns of the used catalyst showed that the structural integrity of the MOF material was maintained after the catalytic reactions. These analytical and characterization data together with the catalytic activity indicate that $\mathbf{MOF{-}253{\cdot}0.05}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{dCl}_{2}$ is stable and reusable.
## Stille coupling
Stille coupling153,154 of aryl halide with alkyl or aryl stannane is a very important reaction that complements the Suzuki–Miyaura cross-coupling for functionalization of aromatic rings. The synthetic utility derives from the possibility to use alkyl and cycloalkyl groups that become attached to the aromatic ring without undergoing rearrangements or consecutive polyalkylation.
The catalytic activity of IRMOF-3-PI-Pd has also been tested in the Stille coupling between bromobenzene and tributylphenyltin to obtain biphenyl (Scheme 29).83 Among the various conditions employed for this reaction, the maximum yield was achieved with LiCl as base in ethanol reaching $88\%$ with a TOF of $2195~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . It appears, however, that LiCl should be considered as a neutral salt rather than a base. The optimized reaction conditions were used to synthesise a series of biphenyl derivatives, although bromobenzene was the only halide employed as a reactant giving a high product yield.83 Chlorobenzene as a substrate afforded only $10\%$ yield of the coupling product. The catalyst was reused five times without any change in the yield. In addition, the stability of IRMOF-3-PI-Pd as a catalyst was supported by the absence of Pd leaching and the absence of detectable changes by powder XRD, IR and gas adsorption in the recovered catalyst compared to the parent catalyst.
## Hantzsch coupling
Dihydropyridines are present in a number of important therapeutic drugs and they exhibit useful biological activities.155–158 Developing a heterogeneous catalytic system for the synthesis of these important compounds is of large interest. In this context, IRMOF-3 has been reported as a heterogeneous catalyst in promoting the Hantzsch coupling leading to dihydropyridines.105 The reaction between benzaldehyde, methyl acetoacetate and ammonium acetate with $4\mathrm{mol\%}$ of IRMOF-3 resulted in $90\%$ yield under solvent-free conditions (Scheme 30). The optimized protocol was further extended to synthesise a series of substituted dihydropyridines in high yields. Leaching and reusability data are necessary to support the heterogeneity of the catalytic system. Furthermore, characterization data of used catalysts are needed to establish the stability of IRMOF-3 under reaction conditions.
The same group has further expanded the utility of IRMOF-3 for the synthesis of polyhydroquinoline (Scheme 31) in moderate to high yields under solvent-free conditions.106 The catalyst was reused four times without change in the yield in the first three runs and observing a yield decrease from 90 to $72\%$ from the third to fourth run. Characterization of the spent catalyst should have given some insights over the reasons of catalyst deactivation.
## C–C coupling reactions
Two metalloporphyrin octacarboxylates (Scheme 32) of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ and ${\bf N i}^{\mathrm{II}}$ were used to link $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ nodes for the formation of two novel porous mixed-metal MOFs, namely, $[\mathrm{Cu}_{16}(\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{OCPP})_{3}.$ $\mathrm{\big(OH\big)}_{11}\big(H_{2}O\big)_{17}\big]\cdot21\mathrm{DMF}\cdot65\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ (ZJU-22; $\mathbf{Mn}^{\mathrm{HII}}{\mathbf{\mathrm{-}}}\mathbf{H_{8}O C P P}=\mathbf{Mn}\bigl(\mathrm{III}\bigr)$
5,10,15,20-tetrakis(3,5-dicarboxylphenyl)porphyrin) and $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}(N i\mathrm{-}}$ $\mathrm{OCPP)(H_{2}O)_{4}]\cdot10D M F\cdot11H_{2}O}$ (ZJU-21).107 The structure of ZJU-22 presents a unique 3D framework constructed from three independent nets interconnected by Mn–OH–Cu bridges defining 1D nanotubular channels of about $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The interconnected nets are expected to significantly stabilize the framework. The BET surface area of ZJU-21 and ZJU-22 were 1339 and $809\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively.
The catalytic activity of these ZJU solids was compared in cross-dehydrogenative coupling reaction. This coupling reaction between 2-phenyl-1,2,3,4-tetrahydroisoquinoline and nitromethane under oxidative conditions was selected as a model reaction to compare the activity of ZJU-21 and ZJU-22 using tert-butylhydroperoxide (TBHP) as an oxidant under solventfree conditions at room temperature (Scheme 33).107 ZJU-22 exhibited higher catalytic activity than ZJU-21 reaching a product yield of 97 and $90\%$ , respectively. The difference in reactivity between ZJU-21 and ZJU-22 is even higher in water, reaching yields of the coupling product of 66 and $93\%$ , respectively. This reaction was still efficiently promoted by ZJU-21 and ZJU-22 when the amount of 2-phenyl-1,2,3,4-tetrahydroisoquinoline was scaled up by twenty-fold. To discriminate the potential activity of Cu nodes versus the metalloporphyrin linkers, the same reaction was carried out using HKUST-1 as a catalyst under identical reaction conditions achieving $76\%$ yield. HKUST-1 has a composition of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ with dimeric $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddle wheel units that can catalyze oxidations with TBHP.159 These data clearly establish the superiority of ZJU-21 and ZJU-22 for this reaction, a fact that is attributable to the activity of the metal porphyrin linker. The absence of metal leaching supports the heterogeneity of the reaction. Powder XRD patterns of the recovered solids confirmed the structural integrity of ZJU-21 and ZJU-22 catalysts. The ZJU-22 catalyst was used for six successive runs without loss of catalytic efficiency.
Copper terephthalate $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{bdc})\right]$ has been reported as a reusable heterogeneous catalyst for the aerobic homocoupling of arylboronic acids to the corresponding symmetrical biphenyls under mild reaction conditions.108 The reaction was optimized with phenylboronic acid as a model substrate, achieving a maximum yield of $97\%$ in DMF at room temperature (Scheme 34). The activity of $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ was also compared with MOF-101, $[\mathrm{Cu(pdc)}_{2}]\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathbf{Me}_{2}$ (pdc: pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylic acid), $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}-$ $\operatorname{ted}]_{n}$ (ndc: 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid; ted: triethylenediamine) and $[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{\prime\prime})]_{n}$ ( $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathbf{L}^{\prime\prime}$ : $(Z)$ -4-((2-hydroxynaphthalen-1- yl)methyleneamino)benzene-1,3-dioic acid) MOFs containing identical amount of copper as in $\mathrm{Cu(bdc)}$ .108 The expected homocoupling product was formed only using MOF-101 and $[\mathrm{Cu(pdc)}_{2}]\cdot$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}$ as catalysts with yields of 76 and $62\%$ , respectively. The lower activity of these two MOFs compared with $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ was attributed to the hindered diffusion of the substrate and products through the pore system of these MOFs. On the other hand, homogeneous catalysts like $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{\cu{Cl}_{2}}$ showed lower yields of 42, 18 and $31\%$ , respectively. The heterogeneity test showed the absence of metal leaching under the experimental conditions. The catalyst was reused five times with a slight decrease in the yield of biphenyl. It was concluded that $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ exhibits higher activity over other conventional catalysts of Cu, Pd, Fe and Au, but, this conclusion has to be taken cautiously when the activity of $\operatorname{cu}({\mathsf{b d c}})$ has been measured under different conditions than those employed with other catalysts. It is also relevant to mention here that it would be convenient to compare the activity of $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ with heterogeneous solids of similar structural characteristics. Powder XRD patterns of the fresh, three and five times reused $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ revealed that the reused catalyst maintains its structural integrity during the catalysis. However, the BET isotherms showed a small decrease in the surface area. This could be due to substrate or product absorption, but also to the presence of inorganic compounds derived from boronic acid.
## C–H to C–C bond forming reactions
The direct transformation of a C–H bond into a C–C bond is an efficient process from the sustainability point of view. In this context, homogeneous Pd catalysts have been reported to promote C2-arylation of indoles with activated arenes.160,161 One drawback associated with these processes is catalyst recovery from the reaction mixture. For this reason, it is always desirable for commercial applications to develop a heterogeneous catalyst.
Recently, Cao and co-workers have prepared Pd NPs $(0.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $2.6\pm0.5\mathrm{{nm}}$ embedded into the pores of MIL-101. The reaction between $N$ -methylindole and iodobenzene to achieve $N\cdot$ methyl2-phenylindole was examined using $\mathbf{Pd/MIL}–101$ as a catalyst (Scheme 35).109 The target product was obtained with $85\%$ yield using CsOAc as base in DMF or dimethylacetamide, without observing C3-arylation. In contrast, the reaction failed with MIL-101 as a promoter or in the presence of $\mathbf{Pd/MIL}–101$ without CsOAc. Furthermore, a commercial Pd/C catalyst $(5\mathrm{wt\%})$ Pd) only gave traces of the C2-arylation product along with $22\%$ of the biphenyl as a byproduct. A low activity of $25\%$ conversion was observed for Pd NPs embedded on the $\mathrm{1IL-53(Al)–NH_{2}}$ catalyst. These data clearly establish the superior activity of MIL101 for the indole coupling with iodobenzene. The enhanced activity of $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-101}$ may be explained from the larger surface area and accessible mesoporous cages of MIL-101, which would be a key factor for the high dispersion of the Pd active sites and facilitates reactant diffusion into the pores. Leaching of Pd was observed to be $0.4~\mathrm{ppm}$ from ICP-AES analysis. The crystallinity and particle size were retained as evidenced by XRD and TEM respectively after using the catalyst five times. On the other hand, low yields were obtained for the reaction of bromobenzene $(31\%)$ and chlorobenzene $(21\%)$ with $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -methylindole. These unsatisfactory yields clearly show that there is still room for development of more active heterogeneous Pd catalysts following the lead of highly active Pd complexes.
Later, the same group developed a new protocol for C2-arylation of various indoles with arylboronic acids using Pd/MIL-101 and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ or a 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine- $.N\cdot$ -oxyl (TEMPO) radical as an external oxidant in the presence of HOAc.110 The reaction between $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -methylindole and phenylboronic acid to obtain $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -methyl-2-phenylindole using $\mathbf{Pd/MIL}–101$ afforded $87\%$ yield (Scheme 35) in $\mathrm{CH_{2}C l_{2}/A c O H}$ using $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as an oxidant. The hotfiltration test showed $0.9\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of Pd leaching. The catalyst was reused for six cycles and no changes were observed in its crystallinity and particle size. Furthermore, this protocol exhibited higher yields for various substrates irrespective of their size, and the presence of electron withdrawing and electrondonating substituents on indole or phenylboronic acid.
Recently, MOF-199 has been reported as a heterogeneous catalyst for the direct oxidative $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ coupling reaction via $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ functionalization between $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylaniline and phenylacetylene to form $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ methyl-N-(3-phenylprop-2-ynyl)benzenamine in $79\%$ yield in the presence of TBHP (Scheme 36).111 MOF-199 exhibited comparable activity to other Cu-MOFs such as $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ , Cu(bdc) and $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}(\mathbf{DABCO})}$ . However Co-MOF-74 and $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}-$ (DABCO) were found to be catalytically inactive for this reaction. The catalyst was recycled ten times without much decline in the yield. It was observed that reused MOF-199 was still highly crystalline.
## C–O coupling reactions
As we have presented earlier, MOF-253, Al(OH)(bpydc), can coordinate $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ and the resulting material is an efficient catalyst for cross-coupling reactions. In other related work, CuI has been immobilized on MOF-253 through non-coordinated bpy moieties and its catalytic activity was tested in the $O$ -arylation of phenols and alcohols with aryl halides under ligand-free conditions.112 The reaction between 4-methoxyiodobenzene with $p$ -cresol in the presence of $20~\mathrm{mol\%}$ MOF-253.0.5CuI exhibited $96\%$ yield of the C–O coupling product (Scheme 37) in DMSO and $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as base. However, 3 and $0\%$ yields of the coupling product were obtained using independent CuI and MOF-253 as catalysts, respectively. These results demonstrate a significant promoting effect of the MOF-253 on the CuI active species due to the formation of the metal bipyridyl complex. Accordingly, $51\%$ yield of the product was obtained when the same amount of bpy was used instead of MOF-253 in the presence of CuI. In an attempt to use aryl chlorides as substrates did not furnish the desired products. Hence, there is still room for further development of a more efficient generation of a MOF-253 catalyst to promote this coupling with aryl chlorides and also to reduce the catalyst substrate molar ratio. MOF-253.0.5CuI also showed very high activity towards the coupling of methanol with iodobenzene in $98\%$ yield (Scheme 37).112 Alcohols with an aromatic (Scheme 37) or cyclic ring (Scheme 37) also underwent $o$ -arylation smoothly under the same reaction conditions.112 Cu leaching was lower than $0.1\%$ of the initial Cu content. XRD of the three-times used catalyst did not show significant changes with respect to that of the fresh catalyst, suggesting that the structural integrity of MOF-253 is maintained during the catalysis.
A crystalline porous $\mathrm{\bfCu}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ has been reported as a heterogeneous catalyst for the cross-dehydrogenative coupling of DMF with 2-substituted phenols to form organic carbamates through $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ activation under oxidative conditions.113 Structural analysis162 reveals that $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(b p d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ possesses an interpenetrated pillared-grid structure based on $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ paddle-wheels with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate, and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine as ligands. The coupling reaction between 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde with DMF in the molar ratio of $1:50$ , in the presence of 1.5 equivalents of TBHP as the oxidant, with 5 mol% $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}}(b p d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ as the catalyst exhibited $97\%$ conversion (Scheme 38).113 Under identical reaction conditions, $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{bpdc})}}$ and $\operatorname{cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ showed lower activity than $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(b p d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ . However, further studies would be necessary to rationalize the different behaviour of these MOFs in this coupling reaction. Heterogeneity test showed that there was no active species dissolved in the solution. The catalyst was reused six times without change in activity until four cycles and then the yield decreased in the fifth run to $84\%$ . This decrease in the yield was accompanied by some changes in the XRD patterns that indicate a slight difference in the crystal structure. However, 2-methylphenol and 2-nitrophenol exhibited lower reactivity with DMF, resulting in 38 and $42\%$ conversions, respectively. Phenol was found to be almost unreactive in the coupling reaction observing only $8\%$ conversion.
In another precedent, the same group has extended their investigation on the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{\bfCu_{2}(b p d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ as a catalyst for the cross-dehydrogenative coupling of ethers with 2-carbonyl-substituted phenols.114 The cross-dehydrogenative coupling between 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 1,4-dioxane using $3\mathrm{mol\%}$ of $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(b p d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ catalyst with the molar ratio of $1:50$ with 3 eq. of TBHP as the oxidant resulted in complete conversion (Scheme 39).114 As in the case of the oxidative coupling of phenols with DMF, $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc}})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu(bdc)}$ and $\mathrm{\cu(bpdc)}$ exhibited low activity. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{{bpy})}}$ catalyst outperforms the activity of conventional homogeneous catalysts like $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{\cucl}$ , CuI, $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ in terms of activity as well as the catalyst reusability. Heterogeneity test showed the absence of metal leaching and $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})$ can be reused for eight runs with only slight decrease in its activity. Powder XRD of the reused $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(b p d c)_{2}(b p y)}$ catalyst indicated that the crystallinity is maintained during the course of the reaction.
Phan and co-workers have reported the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ for the coupling reaction of phenols with nitroarenes by replacing the nitro group. This MOF turned out to be an efficient recoverable catalyst without the need of additional ligands.115 The reaction between 4-nitrobenzaldehyde and phenol with a molar ratio of $1{:}1.5$ in DMF and $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as the base in the presence of $3\mathrm{\mol\%}$ $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ as the catalyst resulted in complete conversion (Scheme 40).115 The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{\bullet{\bfu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ in this coupling reaction was also compared with three of its components namely, $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{c}$ , DABCO and other Cu salts like $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathbf{OAc})_{2},$ and CuI. Even though several homogeneous Cu salts exhibited reasonable activities for the coupling reaction, the use of $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ is still advantageous due to the intrinsic properties of heterogeneous catalysis in terms of easy separation of the catalyst from the reaction mixture and reusability. On the other hand, more than $99\%$ conversion was achieved for the reaction of 4-chlorophenol with 4-nitrobenzaldehyde, whereas the coupling reaction of 4-cyanophenol with 4-nitrobenzaldehyde proceeded slowly with $54\%$ conversion. These results clearly establish the superiority of $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}(\mathbf{DABCO})$ in promoting the reaction of 4-chlorophenol with nitroarene while earlier studies failed to promote the Ullmann reaction with 4-chlorophenol163 or electron-deficient phenols.112,164,165 No leaching of Cu active sites was observed under the reaction conditions. The catalyst was recovered and reused with a conversion of more than $99\%$ up to six runs without a significant degradation of its catalytic activity. Powder of the reused catalyst showed that the crystallinity of $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ was preserved during the catalytic reaction. All together, catalytic data as well as spectroscopic and structural characterization indicate the stability of $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ as a catalyst for this type of nucleophilic substitution.
MOF-199 has been reported as an efficient recyclable solid catalyst for the Ullmann-type reaction between aryl iodides and phenols to form diaryl ethers.116 MOF-199 was synthesised from $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3}){\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and btc as a ligand and its structure resembles that of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ . The reaction was carried out with the 4-iodoacetophenone:phenol molar ratio of 1 : 2 using $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ MOF-199 catalyst in DMF achieving quantitative conversion in the presence of acetylacetone as a ligand and MeONa as a base (Scheme 41).116 A conversion of $63\%$ was observed for the reaction of 4-chlorophenol, while only $8\%$ conversion was reached for the reaction of 4-cyanophenol. However, replacing MeONa by $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as the base, the reaction of 4-chlorophenol and 4-cyanophenol enhanced the reaction rate significantly, reaching conversions of 99 and $60\%$ , respectively. On the other hand, the catalytic activity of MOF-199 was also superior or comparable with some homogeneous catalysts. Heterogeneity test showed the absence of active species in the solution. The catalyst was reused five times with only a slight decrease in its activity. Powder XRD of the reused MOF-199 showed that the crystallinity of the catalyst was retained during the catalysis. Cu leaching in the reused MOF-199 was negligible and TEM also indicated that the porosity of the material was retained, although nitrogen physisorption measurements revealed that the porous structure of the reused MOF-199 was slightly different when compared to that of the fresh catalyst.
Recently, Corma and co-workers have reported the catalytic activity of $\operatorname{Cu}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ (im: imidazole) for oxidative C–O coupling reactions by direct C–H activation of formamides, aldehydes and ethers.117 The reaction between 2-hydroxyacetophenone and DMF in the presence of $5\ \mathrm{mol\%}$ of $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ and TBHP resulted in $93\%$ yield of the $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}$ coupling product. For the sake of comparison, $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ gave quantitative yield of the desired product under the same reaction conditions. However, $\mathrm{Cu}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ can be recovered at the end of the reaction and reused without significant loss of activity for at least 3 catalytic cycles. Interestingly, $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ MOF exhibited better activity by giving $97\%$ yield (Scheme 42) of the product with full phenol conversion in half the time as that required for $\operatorname{Cu}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ . Here again, the crystalline structure was preserved during the reaction and the catalyst was reusable for at least three consecutive reactions. On the other hand, $\mathrm{Cu(bdc)}$ as a catalyst afforded only $71\%$ yield after much longer reaction time. This product yield is much lower than that achieved by $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ as catalysts. Analogously, it was established that the reaction between 2-hydroxyacetophenone and DMF did not yield any product in the presence of other MOFs as catalysts, including Cr-MIL-101 and CPO-27 materials with Mn, Co and Ni as metals. Interestingly, $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZSM}{\cdot}5$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ -USY exhibited 93 and $98\%$ yields, respectively, under identical reaction conditions.
By comparing the activities of these zeolites with MOFs, it can be concluded that $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZSM}{\cdot}5$ has a catalytic activity similar to $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ , but the performance of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ -USY was superior to that of $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ . However, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -USY loses most of its activity in one catalytic cycle due to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ leaching.
In a further development of this oxidative coupling, a tandem reaction system in which in situ formation of the hydroperoxide is combined with the C–O coupling was studied (Scheme 43). The one-pot oxidation of cumene promoted by $5\mathrm{\mol\%}$ $\mathrm{Cu}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ under an $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ atmosphere resulted in the formation of cumene hydroperoxide in about $20\%$ yield, and to these mixtures, subsequent 2-hydroxyacetophenone and DMF was added at the same reaction temperature. The desired carbamate was observed in $97\%$ yield. This study clearly establishes the possibility of using CuMOF as a bifunctional catalyst for a tandem process in which the copper ions of the MOF promotes the aerobic oxidation of cumene to cumene hydroperoxide, which was used as an oxidizing reagent to catalyze the oxidative C–O coupling of a formamide and a 2-carbonyl-substituted phenol by the same Cu active centres.
In another possible oxidative cross $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}$ coupling, the reaction of benzaldehyde and 2-hydroxyacetophenone in the presence of
$5\mathrm{mol\%}$ $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ and TBHP yielded $80\%$ of the 2-acetylphenylbenzoate (Scheme 42).117 Under identical conditions, $\operatorname{Cu}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ showed considerably lower activity affording only $36\%$ yield of this aryl ester. Interestingly, $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ exhibited practically full conversion and full selectivity to the concerned product, even improving the results obtained with $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ . The TOFs measured with $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ of $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ were 4 and $84~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. These large differences between the two CuMOFs in the activity were believed to arise most probably from the different abilities of each Cu-MOF to coordinate and decompose the hydroperoxide and the relative rate of the oxidative C–O coupling reaction. Also in this case, both MOFs were found to be stable under the reaction conditions and can be reused without significant loss of activity.
The oxidative cross-coupling between 2-hydroxyacetophenone and dioxane was also performed with $\operatorname{Cu}(2\mathrm{-pymo})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ as catalysts using TBHP as the oxidant (Scheme 42).117 In this coupling, $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ was found to be more active than $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ , resulting in $82\%$ yield of the desired product in the presence of $\operatorname{Cu}(2\cdot\operatorname{pymo})_{2}$ , while $73\%$ yield was obtained with $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ under identical conditions.117 Again, $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ exhibited the best activity by providing $85\%$ yield of the coupling product. Both Cu-MOFs were stable under reaction conditions and both can be fairly reused. However, $\operatorname{Cu}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ started to lose its activity significantly after the ${4}^{\mathrm{th}}$ run, which was associated with a gradual loss in crystallinity and adsorption of the reaction products on the surface of the catalyst. In contrast, $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{{im}})_{2}$ showed fairly high stability and activity for at least six catalytic cycles, thus, reaching a cumulative TON of 98 moles of the product formed per mol of Cu which compares favourably with the maximum TON value of 14.6 attained with the non-recoverable $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ catalyst.
## C–N coupling
The reaction between $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{p}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{t}$ and $\mathrm{CuSO_{4}{\cdot}5H_{2}O}$ resulted in two novel dodecanuclear Cu metallomacrocycle-based 3D MOFs $\mathbb{[\mathrm{[Cu_{4}(p b t)_{2}.}}$ $(\mathrm{SO_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}(\mathrm{CH_{3}O H})]{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMF}]_{n}$ and $\mathrm{\small{[[Cu_{2}(p b t)(S O_{4})(D M S O)-}}$ $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\right]\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}]_{n}$ .118 The catalytic activity of these two MOFs was tested in the oxidative coupling at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ of phenylacetylene and oxazolidin-2-one (Scheme 44). The expected colourless product 3-(2-phenylethynyl)oxazolidin-2-one was obtained in a yield of $78\%$ using $[[\mathrm{Cu_{4}(p b t)_{2}(S O_{4})_{2}(D M F)_{2}(C H_{3}O H)}]\cdot7\mathrm{H_{2}O}\cdot D\mathrm{MF}]_{n}$ and $66\%$ with $[[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{pbt})(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{DMSO})(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{2}]\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}]_{n}$ as catalysts. Interestingly, these two complexes exhibited higher catalytic activity than that of CuBr previously reported in the literature for this oxidative C–N coupling reaction.166 However, there are no data ruling out the occurrence of Cu leaching or comparing the powder XRD patterns of fresh and reused catalysts. In addition, the possibility that the two Cu MOFs can be utilized as effective heterogeneous catalysts must be supported with catalytic data showing their reusability.
A Cu-carboxylate MOF has been prepared from 4,4-dibenzoic acid and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ The catalytic activity of this air-stable MOF complex was tested in the oxidative $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{N}$ cross-coupling reaction of phenylacetylene with 2-oxazolidone.119 Powder XRD patterns indicated the formation of Cu-carboxylate as a crystalline MOF. The reaction between phenylacetylene and 2-oxazolidone proceeded at ${70~^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ to give the target ynamide product in $49\%$ yield with a significant amount of phenylacetylene dimer in $35\%$ yield. The formation of the dimer was reduced drastically by performing the reaction at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to give the ynamide in $71\%$ yield (Scheme 44). Although the Cu-carboxylate MOF can be recovered from the reaction mixture, reusability tests showed poor activity. Thus, the catalyst activity decreased from 71 to $53\%$ from $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ to $5^{\mathrm{th}}$ run, respectively. This reduction in the yield was assumed to be due to the significant damage of the catalyst framework caused by the high temperature required for promoting the reaction. It would have been advisable to compare XRD of the fresh and used samples, as well as to obtain information on possible metal leaching.
## $A^{3}$ coupling
Propargylamines are considered one of the key intermediates in the preparation of many nitrogen-containing biologically active heterocyclic compounds, such as $\upbeta$ -lactams and oxotremorine analogues, as well as common motifs in natural products and therapeutic drugs.167,168 The interest in the synthesis of these propargylamines lies on the versatile nature of these substituted amines as intermediates in organic synthesis.169,170 A general process to obtain propargylamines is based on the catalytic coupling of an aldehyde, an amine and a terminal alkyne that undergoes C–H activation, water being considered to be one of the byproducts (Scheme 45). This reaction is often called $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ coupling. Recently, we have reviewed the various catalytic systems based on MOFs which have been employed as heterogeneous catalysts for the preparation of propargylamines among other N-containing compounds.39 Hence, the reader is referred to this article for a comprehensive review of the use of MOFs in the synthesis of propargylamines through the $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ coupling reaction. Gold functionalized MOFs have also been recently reported as a heterogeneous catalyst for the synthesis of propargylamine.171
Among the most recent studies in a multicomponent reaction similar to $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ coupling, it has been reported that $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}I_{2}}(b t t p_{4})}$ $\mathrm{(bttp_{4})}$ : benzene-1,3,5-trityl triisonicotinate) is a suitable catalyst for the coupling of tosyl azide, phenylacetylene and diisopropylamine (Scheme 46) and can be recycled.172
## Concluding remarks
As we have shown in this review, MOFs have a large flexibility to develop catalytic systems to promote cross-coupling reactions. We have shown examples where the metal nodes have free coordination positions that can be active to promote this type of reactions, such as $\mathbf{Pd}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ . In this case, however, the nature of the metal is limited to Pd, Cu or some other transition metal ion with catalytic activity in organometallic coordination chemistry. It appears, however, that the mechanism of coupling reactions, where oxidation and reduction and continuous cycling in the oxidation state of the metal atom is taking place, should exert considerable stress on to the framework of MOFs. Due to this drawback and following the lead of homogeneous catalysis, a second strategy to develop MOF-based catalysts for cross-coupling reactions has been to use the organic linker as a ligand for $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ or other active metal ions. This strategy is really corresponding to the heterogenization of a soluble $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ complex on an insoluble solid using the organic component of the MOF as a ligand. Another possibility that is probably the most appealing due to its simplicity is to include small metal NPs within the pores of the MOFs. In this case, the ligand could have or not some substituent such as amino groups to interact with the NPs to stabilize their size and impede their leaching and growth. It appears that when Pd NP size is small the activity of Pd NPs occluded in MOFs compares favorably with the most active heterogeneous catalysts ever reported. These three possible methodologies illustrate the flexibility that MOFs offer in the design of cross-coupling catalysts and in combination with the design of appropriate organic ligands and suitable transition metals can also easily lead to the development of bifunctional solid catalysts.
With respect to the target in the area of MOFs as crosscoupling catalysts, the general goal is to develop cross-coupling catalysts for the widest possible range of substrates particularly aryl chlorides that could be reusable and perfectly stable under the reaction conditions. One of the prerequisites to achieve stability is the use of robust MOFs that should be stable to withstand most types of solvents and aggressive reagents as well as harsh reaction conditions. Another target is to make efficient use of precious metals, meaning to achieve very high TON values. Among the various possibilities to increase the catalytic activity, one that surely will be explored in the future is the use of nano alloys of Pd with other non critical metals. These nano alloys could have a core–shell or random structure and by modification of the electronic densities of Pd should increase its chemical reactivity, particularly for challenging crosscoupling reactions.
Overall the final target in this area is to show the superiority of MOF-based catalysts for cross-coupling reactions over other solid catalysts and this has to be evidenced by application of these materials as catalysts in the synthesis of important commercial compounds and drugs on an industrial scale replacing the current homogeneous catalysts.
